<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cornelis Speelman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1628">1628</a>–<a href="/years/1684">1684</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cornelis Speelman was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> commander and governor-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> from 1681 to 1684. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arung-palakka">Arung Palakka</a> of Bone, he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-hasanuddin">Sultan Hasanuddin</a> of Gowa and imposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bongaya">Treaty of Bongaya</a> of 1667, which ended Makassar's power and its free trade. He later campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a>'s rebellion on behalf of the Mataram court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cornelis-speelman.jpg" alt="Cornelis Speelman" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Martin Palin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Cornelis_Speelman_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Speelman was born on 2 March 1628, the son of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a> merchant, and in his 16th year sailed for the Indies aboard the Hillegersberg as an assistant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> (VOC). He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> in 1645, became bookkeeper in 1648 and second merchant in 1649, and went as secretary with the ambassador Joan Cunaeus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, where Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-ii-of-persia">Abbas II</a> received them with great festivity; he wrote an account of the voyage. He married Petronella Maria Wonderaer, the fifteen-year-old daughter of the receiver-general.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> he became bookkeeper-general in 1657, head of the company's clerical staff in 1659 and a schepen, a kind of alderman, of Batavia in 1661. Appointed governor and director of Dutch Coromandel on 12 June 1663, he was suspended for illegal private trading, having bought a diamond for his wife and resold it when she disliked it, and was sentenced to a 15 months suspension and a fine of 3,000 guilders. In 1666 he was named admiral of an expedition to Makassar, and on 18 November 1667 he concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bongaya">Treaty of Bongaya</a>.</p><p>A second expedition in 1669 completed the subjugation of Makassar, for which he received a gold chain and medallion. He became a full Counsellor of the Indies in 1671 and commanded a fleet against the French the next year. From December 1676 he led an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a> in support of the ruler of Mataram and fought the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> on the east coast until his recall at the end of 1677. Made Director-General of the Indies in 1678 and governor-general on 29 October 1680, he took up office on 25 November 1681, succeeding Rijckloff van Goens.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During his term the sultan of Ternate was defeated and ceded all the lands of his kingdom to the company, and Speelman also subdued the city of Bantam. He died in the Castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> on 11 January 1684 and was buried in the Kruiskerk with great splendour, to a salute of 229 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> shots. Johannes Camphuys succeeded him as governor-general.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cornelis Speelman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Dutch East India Company under <b>Cornelis Speelman</b>, allied with Arung Palakka of Bone, defeated Sultan Hasanuddin, and the Treaty of Bongaya of 1667 ended Gowa's power; Somba Opu fell in 1669.</blockquote><a href="/state/gowa-sultanate">Gowa Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.92,4.48&amp;year=1664" aria-label="Rotterdam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Cornelis Speelman"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5236 5681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5236 5681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.92,4.48&amp;year=1664">What was here in 1664? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
